    text: [
      "The southern expedition travelled through forests that grew increasingly unfamiliar with every passing day. The trees stood close together, their branches forming thick canopies that allowed only narrow shafts of sunlight to reach the ground. The Vanaras crossed streams, climbed rocky ridges, and followed animal trails through regions where no familiar settlement could be seen.",
      "Angada maintained the pace of the expedition, but he was careful not to exhaust the warriors. Jambavan frequently advised the younger members to observe the terrain before choosing a route. A narrow passage between two hills might appear shorter, but it could lead into a valley from which there was no easy return.",
      "Hanuman moved ahead whenever the path required closer examination. He studied broken branches, disturbed soil, and the signs left by travellers and animals. He also questioned forest dwellers whenever the expedition encountered them. Most had heard nothing of Sita, but Hanuman never dismissed a conversation simply because it produced no immediate answer.",
      "One evening, the party stopped beside a stream. Angada looked across the darkening forest and asked Jambavan whether they were moving in the right direction. The elder replied that direction alone was not enough. They needed evidence, and evidence could sometimes be found only after patience and repeated examination.",
      "The warriors rested beneath the trees. Their search had begun with the strength of a great kingdom behind them. Now they were learning that the wilderness did not respond to strength alone."
    ],
    emotion: "Determination tested by distance and uncertainty."
